--TEST--
A field of `Core\Task::all`'s result accepts a write of the type that field's own callable declared, and reads it back — `rule:concurrency/all-answers-a-typed-shape`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The result carries the *argument object's* class, because a shape class is
// named for its field names alone and both sides spell the same ones — so the
// slot tags that class promises would be the object's, where every field
// holds a callable, and every write below would be refused for writing an `int`
// to a field declared `object`. `nvs_ir::lower::Lowering::record_core_result_shape`
// records the result's own representations against that same label instead,
// which degrades each disagreeing slot to unchecked — `nvs_runtime::object`'s
// module doc § *What a shape write checks*, case 4.
//
// Two fields answering *different* types, and both written: a member that kept
// one side's tags refuses on the first write, and one that degraded only the
// slot it happened to see refuses on the second.
var $page = Core\Task::all({
    count: fn (): int    => 1,
    label: fn (): string => "a",
});
$page->count = 5;
$page->label = "b";
echo $page->count, " ", $page->label, "\n";

// The read side never depended on the tag — `SlotGet` keys on the name — so
// the awaited values are still what a fresh group answers, and writing over
// them is what the lines above proved. Asked of a second group so the first
// one's writes cannot be what this prints.
var $again = Core\Task::all({
    count: fn (): int    => 7,
    label: fn (): string => "c",
});
echo $again->count, " ", $again->label, "\n";
--EXPECT--
5 b
7 c
